<pdd-reason>Records the callable signature of every public symbol with a binding-kind marker, so a signature or binding change is detected even when the parameter list looks unchanged.</pdd-reason>
<pdd-interface>
{
  "type": "module",
  "module": {
    "functions": [
      {"name": "_snapshot_public_signatures", "signature": "(code_text: str, language: str, file_path: str = None) -> Dict[str, str]", "returns": "Dict[str, str]"},
      {"name": "_format_python_signature", "signature": "(node: ast.AST, *, skip_first: bool = False) -> str", "returns": "str"},
      {"name": "_resolve_class_node", "signature": "(tree: ast.Module, dotted_name: str) -> Optional[ast.ClassDef]", "returns": "Optional[ast.ClassDef]"}
    ]
  }
}
</pdd-interface>

% You are an expert Python engineer. Implement the public-signature snapshotter in
% `pdd/conformance/signatures.py`.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Responsibility
  Mapping each public symbol to a signature entry, so the gate can catch a
  callable whose shape changed while its name stayed. Static analysis only.

% Non-responsibilities
  Does not compare entries - callers do that semantically via
  `pdd.interface_semantics`. Does not decide removals.

% Vocabulary
  - **entry**: the recorded string for one symbol, always prefixed by its
    binding kind.
  - **binding kind**: how the name is bound - plain function, async function,
    class, instance/static/class method, property, module assignment, or import
    re-export.

% Contract rules
  R1. The set of public names MUST be identical to `surface.py`'s, applying the
      same `__all__` precedence, class-member recursion, patch-target
      preservation, re-export-only import rule and `__future__` exclusion. If the
      two disagree, the removed-symbol diff and the signature diff contradict each
      other. Reuse `surface.py`'s helpers rather than reimplementing the rules.
  R2. Non-Python input MUST produce an empty mapping.
  R3. Every entry MUST begin with its binding kind, so a flip is observable even
      when the receiver-stripped parameter list matches. Without it, a caller
      doing `Class.f(1)` breaks silently when `def f(self, x)` becomes
      `@staticmethod def f(x)`. The markers are `[function]`, `[async_function]`,
      `[class]`, `[instance]`, `[staticmethod]`, `[classmethod]`,
      `[property:<roles>]`, `[assignment]`, and the import forms below.
  R4. Import re-exports MUST record enough to detect a re-point: `[import]` for a
      plain `import pathlib`; `[import:<module>]` for a renaming
      `import pathlib as p`; `[import:from <module>]` for a plain
      `from pathlib import Path`; `[import:from <module>:<source>]` for a
      renaming `from pathlib import Path as P`.
  R5. A REDUNDANT alias binds the same object as the plain form and MUST
      canonicalise to the plain marker, never the renaming form. Otherwise
      normalising `from pathlib import Path as Path` into
      `from pathlib import Path` under a stable `__all__` diffs as a phantom
      change.
  R6. A relative import's recorded module MUST include its level, so a re-export
      moved from `from . import Foo as Foo` to `from .. import Foo as Foo` diffs
      instead of colliding on an empty module name.
  R7. Only PUBLIC imports may reach the mapping - those a clean `__all__` lists,
      or redundant aliases. Skipping this filter lets a non-public import flip to
      a same-named `def` and false-positive, even though the surface set correctly
      dropped it (#1662, #1663, pdd_cloud#2256). `__future__` imports are excluded
      for the same reason as in the surface: a regenerated module that drops the
      directive and binds a real `annotations` value would otherwise diff as an
      import becoming an assignment.
  R8. A property's entry MUST reflect ALL accessors that exist, accumulated per
      property name. Without that merge a setter overwrites the getter entry, and
      replacing the descriptor with a plain method could produce an identical
      string. Accessor decorators MUST be classified before the general
      attribute-decorator case, or `@x.setter` is flattened to a plain method.
  R9. A class's entry MUST be its constructor ABI: its explicit `__init__` when
      present, otherwise the synthesised `@dataclass` constructor.
  R10. Class members MUST be keyed by dotted name at every depth, matching the
       surface set - a method on `Outer.Inner` is `Outer.Inner.method`.
  R11. `_format_python_signature` MUST preserve parameter order, positional-only
       and keyword-only markers, annotations and defaults as written.
       `skip_first` drops a leading `self`/`cls` so a method compares against a
       receiver-stripped declaration.

% Dependencies
  Standard library `ast`; the public-set helpers from `.surface`; and the
  constructor synthesiser from `.dataclass_signatures`.
